(function () {
  window.CCA = window.CCA || {};
  window.CCA.lessonsNew = window.CCA.lessonsNew || [];
  window.CCA.scenarios = window.CCA.scenarios || {};
  window.CCA.why = window.CCA.why || {};
  window.CCA.questions = window.CCA.questions || [];
  function S(id, d, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "single", stem: stem, options: options, correct: [correct], explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }
  function M(id, d, pick, stem, options, correct, explanation, scenario) { var q = { id: id, domain: d, type: "multi", pick: pick, stem: stem, options: options, correct: correct, explanation: explanation, reviewed: false }; if (scenario) q.scenario = scenario; return q; }
  window.CCA.lessonsNew.push(
    {"id": "comm-1", "n": 1, "domain": "comm", "title": "Structured discovery: from a vague ask to testable requirements", "body": "Most Claude projects begin with a sentence such as \"we want AI in support\". Discovery turns that into a problem statement, a baseline and requirements that can be tested. The method is ordinary solution-architecture practice; Anthropic's own testing guidance adds the Claude-specific habit of defining success criteria and an evaluation set before building.\n\nStart with the business outcome and who owns it, then map stakeholders: sponsor, users, operations, security, compliance and data owners. Each group has different constraints. Ask what the work costs today, how it is measured, what a wrong answer costs in each category, where the data lives and who controls it, and which rules apply, such as data residency or audit.\n\nConvert slogans into measurable criteria. \"Accurate and fast\" becomes a pass rate on a labelled sample and a percentile response time at an expected load. Where no baseline exists, have domain experts label a sample of real cases; that gives a human baseline and the seed of the eval set. Record dependencies, assumptions and open risks with owners and dates. When feasibility is unclear, run a short proof of concept with pass criteria agreed beforehand. Conflicting priorities are surfaced and ranked by the accountable sponsor, not settled by whoever is loudest.", "points": ["Begin with the business problem, its owner and a measured baseline, not with a model or a demo.", "Map every stakeholder group, because security, compliance and data owners add constraints that change the architecture.", "Turn adjectives into metrics, thresholds and conditions that can be passed or failed.", "Where no baseline exists, have domain experts label a sample to create a human baseline and a first eval set.", "Record dependencies, assumptions and risks with an owner and date, and sequence delivery around data that is available.", "Use a time-boxed proof of concept with pre-agreed criteria to resolve feasibility doubts."], "traps": ["Adopting a stakeholder's slogan, such as 99.9 percent accurate, as the requirement without asking what errors cost.", "Starting from a demo or the most capable model before the problem and success measures are agreed.", "Committing to targets from vendor benchmarks alone, or after seeing pilot results, instead of testing against the client's own data.", "Letting a dependency on another department stay unowned because it is outside the team."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests", "https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents"], "code": "Vague ask      : add AI to claims triage\nProblem        : handlers spend 12 min per claim on triage\nBaseline       : experts label 300 recent claims\nRequirement    : >= 92% correct category on that set\nNon-functional : p95 under 8 s at 40 concurrent users\nDependency     : policy system has no API (owner, date)\nOpen risk      : data must stay in one region"},
    {"id": "comm-2", "n": 2, "domain": "comm", "title": "Communicating architecture decisions and trade-offs to different audiences", "body": "An architect's decisions only land if stakeholders understand what was chosen, what it gives up and what evidence supports it. The same decision, such as routing simple requests to a smaller model, needs different framing for finance, security, operations and the sponsor. Claude-specific facts are the inputs: model choice trades quality, latency and cost, and Anthropic's guidance is to test candidates on your own tasks rather than rely on generic rankings.\n\nPresent two or three real options, each with measured results on the agreed evaluation, the latency users would feel and the cost per case, then recommend one and name the requirement that decided it. State assumptions and uncertainty openly, such as sample size. For cost, give a range built from volume scenarios and token use measured on a sample, never a single falsely precise figure.\n\nRecord significant choices in a decision record with context, options, criteria and accepted consequences. Explain limitations in business terms: how often a failure occurs in testing, how it is detected and what the fallback is. A deployed model does not update itself from usage, and by default Anthropic does not train on commercial customer content, so say plainly that improvement comes from a resourced review and re-test cycle. If new evidence overturns a decision, bring it back openly with the effect on cost, schedule and risk.", "points": ["Frame each decision in the audience's terms: unit cost for finance, controls for security, runbooks for operations, outcomes for the sponsor.", "Show alternatives and what each gives up; a single option is a sales pitch, not a trade-off.", "Back recommendations with measured results on the project's own eval set, not parameter counts or leaderboards.", "Give cost as a range from scenarios and sampled token use, with assumptions listed and a review point.", "Capture decisions as records of context, options, criteria and consequences so later teams can judge them.", "Revisit decisions openly when evidence changes; silent switches and defended mistakes both erode trust."], "traps": ["Presenting best-case demo figures or leaving out risks to keep confidence high.", "Telling a sponsor that the system will learn from every conversation when the deployed model does not update itself from usage.","Treating model choice as an engineering detail rather than a cost, speed and quality decision stakeholders care about.", "Quoting a single precise monthly cost from list prices and an assumed volume."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/choosing-a-model", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/optimizing-for-cost-and-intelligence", "https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents"]},
    {"id": "comm-3", "n": 3, "domain": "comm", "title": "Feedback loops, expectation management and SLAs", "body": "Stakeholder alignment is a continuing process, not a kickoff event. A flawless demo sets an expectation that production will not match, so show representative and hard cases from the eval set, failures included, and agree what \"good enough\" means. Report pilot results against every agreed reference, including the human baseline and the target, and let the decision owner accept or reject with a plan for any gap.\n\nService-level commitments need care. Each metric needs a definition, a measurement method, a scope and exclusions. Availability depends on upstream services: the Claude API has rate limits per organisation and tier and can return 429 or overloaded errors, so response-time commitments should be conditional on agreed volume and backed by retries, queueing and capacity headroom checked with the provider. A quality objective is best tied to a labelled evaluation set, a threshold and a refresh cadence. Contract wording itself belongs to Legal.\n\nA feedback loop needs three parts: capture, such as a flag linked to the logged request; expert sampling of outputs against a rubric; and an owner with a cadence who turns findings into prompt, retrieval or eval changes that are tested before release. Scope requests go into one prioritised backlog, and incidents follow an agreed escalation path with impact, mitigation and a root-cause review.", "points": ["Reset expectations with representative results and failures, and agree an acceptance line early.", "Write pilot success and exit criteria at kickoff so that sponsors cannot disagree afterwards about what success meant.", "Define every SLA metric with measurement, scope and exclusions, and account for upstream rate limits.", "Make response-time commitments conditional on volume and back them with retry, queueing and headroom.", "Close the feedback loop with capture, expert sampling and an owner who ships tested changes.", "Run scope through one prioritised backlog and incidents through an agreed escalation path."], "traps": ["Accepting availability and accuracy figures as written, or dropping accuracy because it is hard to define.", "Promising latency based on the best observed test result rather than expected load and percentiles.", "Applying prompt changes automatically from negative ratings without regression testing.", "Fixing an incident quietly or waiting for the monthly report to tell affected stakeholders."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/rate-limits", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/errors", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests"]},
    {"id": "comm-4", "n": 4, "domain": "comm", "title": "Documenting architectures and giving implementation guidance", "body": "Documentation is how a design survives the team that built it. Write for the reader's task. Executives need a context view and the value story; engineers need component and data-flow views; operators need a runbook; new developers need worked how-to guides. Layered diagrams with a stated audience and key keep one system understandable at several levels.\n\nFor a Claude-based system, the release unit is the prompt, the model ID and its parameters together. Document where each lives in version control, the current version, and the evaluation results it passed, plus the eval set and scoring method with the latest baseline. Anthropic notes that API model IDs are fixed snapshots, so recording the exact ID and its lifecycle status is part of the design record. Add known limitations and failure modes with mitigations, and the rejected alternatives with reasons, so later teams do not repeat experiments.\n\nFor privacy review, give the Data Privacy Officer a data-flow description: what reaches the model, where it is stored or logged, retention and owners. Legal conclusions are theirs, not the architect's. Shared implementation guidance works best as a few concrete standards, such as server-side key handling, timeouts and retries, logging fields and the prompt change process, with a reference implementation. Keep documents in the repository and update them in the same change that alters behaviour.", "points": ["Match each document to its reader: context diagram, component view, runbook, how-to guide.", "Version prompt, model ID and parameters together and record the evals they passed.", "Hand over the eval set, scoring method and baseline so that operators can judge whether a change is safe.", "Record known limitations and rejected alternatives with reasons.", "Prepare a data-flow description for the Data Privacy Officer and leave compliance conclusions to Legal and Compliance.", "Keep docs with the code and update them in the same change that alters behaviour."], "traps": ["Handing over only source code or a chronological design history and calling it documentation.", "Giving one dense diagram to every audience.", "Asserting compliance in a design document instead of asking the DPO and Legal.", "Handing over personal credentials, or a raw chat history, in place of a structured pack."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/model-ids-and-versions", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/model-deprecations", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/overview"]},
    {"id": "comm-5", "n": 5, "domain": "comm", "title": "Lifecycle: discovery, design, handoff, monitoring and iteration", "body": "A Claude solution passes through discovery, design, build and evaluate, handoff, monitoring and iteration, and eventually retirement. Each phase needs an owner and an exit check. Evaluation criteria are drafted early, in discovery and design, because they define what done means. A pilot moves to production when acceptance criteria are met on the eval set, operational readiness exists (monitoring, runbook, rollback) and a named owner signs off.\n\nHandoff transfers knowledge and ownership: a runbook walkthrough, named owners for monitoring, on-call support and model migration, and the eval set. Anthropic publishes model statuses (active, legacy, deprecated, retired) and retirement dates, and retired models fail requests, so the runbook should record the model ID, its status and an owner who will test a migration against the evals before the date. Aliases, where they exist for earlier models, only point to the latest dated snapshot of a minor version and do not remove that work.\n\nIn operation, monitor more than uptime. Sample live answers against the rubric, check retrieval after each data refresh, and watch error, rate-limit and cost trends. Re-evaluate whenever the prompt, model, retrieval data or tools change, and on a schedule, since inputs drift. Treat a new model release as a change: run evals, compare quality, latency and cost, canary traffic and keep rollback ready. Prioritise iteration by expected effect on agreed measures, using error analysis of logged failures, and plan retirement of the whole system, covering data, dependants, keys and records.", "points": ["Draft evaluation criteria in discovery and design, before building.", "Gate production on eval results, operational readiness and a named owner.", "At handoff, assign owners for monitoring, support and model migration, and record the model ID and retirement date.", "Monitor answer quality and retrieval after data refreshes, not only availability and cost.", "Re-evaluate on any change to prompt, model, data or tools, and on a regular cadence.", "Prioritise iteration by impact on agreed measures and plan retirement of data, keys and dependants."], "traps": ["Assuming a successful launch evaluation covers all later changes.", "Letting an alias or the provider handle retirements without testing a replacement.", "Relying on error rates alone when degraded answers still return success statuses.", "Upgrading to a new model at once because it is newer, without running your own evals."], "sources": ["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/model-deprecations", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/upgrade-between-model-versions", "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests"]}
  );

  window.CCA.scenarios.scCM1 = {"title": "Claims triage assistant at an insurer", "text": "An insurer wants Claude to read inbound claim emails, assign a category and urgency, and draft a summary for a handler. The head of claims expects very high accuracy and near-instant results. Compliance wants an audit trail and some human review, and IT must keep data in one region. Nobody has measured how accurately staff triage claims today, and a pilot is due in eight weeks."};
  window.CCA.scenarios.scCM2 = {"title": "Handing over a knowledge assistant to operations", "text": "A delivery team has built a retrieval-based knowledge assistant for a client and is handing it to the client's operations team. The assistant uses a pinned Claude model that Anthropic has just listed as deprecated, and the client's documents are refreshed in bulk every quarter. The operations staff have not run LLM systems before, and the original team rolls off in two weeks."};

  window.CCA.questions.push(
    S("cm001", "comm", "A client asks a team to \"add AI to customer support\". Which first discovery step is most likely to lead to a useful solution?", ["Agree the business problem and success measures with stakeholders, including today's handle time", "Choose the most capable Claude model and build a demo to see how stakeholders react to it", "Compile every AI feature that competitors advertise and rank them by how often each appears", "Draft the system prompt first so the team can start iterating on real outputs from day one"], 0, "Discovery starts from the business problem and a measurable baseline, so later design, evaluation and value claims have something to anchor to (general practice)."),
    S("cm002", "comm", "In a requirements workshop for the triage assistant, the head of claims says it must be \"99.9% accurate and instant\". What is the best response?", ["Ask what an error costs per claim category and what instant means per user, then set targets", "Record both figures as written and select the largest available model so the targets have the best chance of being met", "Explain that language models cannot reach those numbers and propose a smaller scope without discussing the options", "Defer all targets until after the pilot, then derive them from whatever results the pilot happens to produce"], 0, "Slogans such as \"accurate\" and \"instant\" must be grounded in cost of error and user need, then expressed as measurable, negotiable targets (general practice).", "scCM1"),
    M("cm003", "comm", 2, "An architect is scoping a document-triage assistant. Which TWO discovery questions are most likely to surface constraints that change the architecture?", ["In which regions may the data be processed, and who must audit decisions?", "How should the results dashboard be styled to match the client's brand guidelines?", "How is this work done today, and how are handle time and errors measured?", "Which vendor supplied the client's previous customer relationship system, and when?", "Which stakeholders should be invited to the weekly status meeting this quarter?"], [0, 2], "Residency, audit and the current measured process are discovery questions that change architecture; cosmetic or administrative questions do not (general practice)."),
    S("cm004", "comm", "Interviews show that operations wants maximum speed while the risk team wants every output reviewed by a person. What should the architect do?", ["Build for whichever group is more senior or more vocal and inform the other group at launch time", "Average the two positions by reviewing a fixed share of outputs, chosen without reference to risk", "Design two complete systems in parallel and let users pick the one they prefer after go-live has happened", "Make the conflict explicit, quantify each concern, and take ranked options to the sponsor to decide"], 3, "Conflicting stakeholder needs are resolved by making the trade-off visible and having the accountable sponsor decide on evidence (general practice)."),
    S("cm005", "comm", "Which statement is the best-formed non-functional requirement for a summarisation feature?", ["Summaries should appear quickly for all users, including during busy periods, without noticeable delay", "The system must use the fastest Claude model so that response time is as low as it can be", "The 95th-percentile time to the first summary is under eight seconds at the expected peak of forty concurrent users", "Latency will be reviewed after launch, and any user who complains will be given priority treatment"], 2, "A testable non-functional requirement states metric, threshold and conditions rather than adjectives or implementation choices (general practice)."),
    S("cm006", "comm", "A discovery workshop reveals that a needed data source has no API and belongs to a department that is not part of the project. What is the architect's best action?", ["Assume access will be sorted out later and carry on with the design as it was first planned", "Plan to scrape the data in a later phase and keep that approach out of the main documentation", "Remove the source from scope without telling stakeholders, to protect the delivery date in the plan", "Log it as a dependency and risk with a named owner and date, and plan release one around available data"], 3, "Dependencies outside the team's control must be recorded with owners and dates, and delivery should be sequenced around available inputs (general practice)."),
    S("cm007", "comm", "The insurer has never measured how accurately staff triage claims. What should happen before accuracy targets are committed?", ["Quote a vendor benchmark score for the chosen model and use it as the contractual target figure", "Have domain experts label a sample of recent real claims to set a human baseline and the first eval set", "Commit to one hundred percent so that the sponsor sees ambition and is not disappointed later on", "Ask the model to grade its own outputs and treat the grades as ground truth"], 1, "A human-labelled sample gives a baseline and eval seed before targets are set, in line with Anthropic's guidance on defining success criteria and building evals.", "scCM1"),
    S("cm008", "comm", "A product owner asks whether Claude can handle a task inside a regulated process. The architect is unsure. What is the best next step?", ["Confirm that it can, because current models handle most business tasks well enough", "Run a short, time-boxed proof of concept on representative data with pass criteria agreed beforehand", "Decline the request, because regulated processes are outside what language models should do", "Rely on public demos by other firms in the same sector to judge whether the task is feasible"], 1, "Uncertainty about feasibility is best resolved by a bounded experiment with pre-agreed criteria (general practice)."),
    M("cm009", "comm", 2, "Which TWO items should a requirements record for an LLM solution contain so that later changes can be traced and assessed?", ["Transcripts of every test prompt the team has tried", "The business goal each requirement supports", "The brand colours chosen for the user interface", "An acceptance criterion and an owner for each requirement", "A list of everyone who attended each workshop"], [1, 3], "Traceability needs a link to the goal and a testable criterion with an owner for each requirement (general practice)."),
    S("cm010", "comm", "A sponsor assumes the assistant will \"learn from every conversation\" and improve by itself. How should the architect align expectations?", ["Confirm the assumption, since a larger stored history of conversations makes the model smarter for later users", "Say it will improve automatically once it has gathered about six months of live production usage data", "Explain that the model does not learn from usage; gains need a resourced review and re-test cycle", "Avoid the topic until go-live, so that the sponsor stays enthusiastic about the project in the meantime"], 2, "A deployed Claude model does not update itself from your traffic, and Anthropic states it does not train on commercial inputs or outputs by default; quality improves through a managed loop of review, change and re-evaluation, which must be planned (Anthropic documentation plus general practice)."),
    S("cm011", "comm", "An architect must present the model choice to a non-technical sponsor. Which approach works best?", ["Compare parameter counts and public leaderboard positions to demonstrate technical rigour to the room", "Present only the recommended model so that the meeting stays short and focused on a single choice", "Provide a long appendix of architecture detail and let the sponsor identify any issues to raise", "Show each option's quality on the project's own cases, speed and cost per case, then recommend one with reasons"], 3, "Decision communication should translate options into the sponsor's outcome terms and end with a recommendation (general practice)."),
    S("cm012", "comm", "Which element makes an architecture decision record most useful to a team that joins a year later?", ["Only the final choice, written briefly so that the log stays easy to scan", "The context, the options considered, the criteria used and the consequences accepted", "A list of meeting attendees and the date each person approved the decision", "Links to vendor marketing pages that describe the benefits of the chosen product"], 1, "A useful decision record captures context, options, criteria and consequences, not just the outcome (general practice)."),
    S("cm013", "comm", "A sponsor wants an agent to approve refunds with no human review. Pilot data suggest errors cluster in high-value refunds. What is the best way to communicate the recommendation?", ["Refuse to proceed unless every refund is reviewed, and cite general industry opinion on the risks of AI", "Show error rates and costs by value band, and propose review above a threshold that can loosen", "Implement the autonomous design as requested and add a review step later if problems start to appear", "Tell the sponsor that automated approval is technically impossible with any of the current models"], 1, "Evidence from the pilot, framed in the sponsor's terms, supports a graduated control with a clear path to relax it (general practice)."),
    M("cm014", "comm", 2, "Which TWO practices make a trade-off presentation more trustworthy to stakeholders?", ["State the assumptions and uncertainty behind each figure, such as sample size and data used", "Present a single option so the audience is not confused by alternatives", "Use the best-case figures from the demo so the value is easy to see", "Leave out known risks so the audience stays confident in the plan", "Show what each option gives up as well as what it delivers"], [0, 4], "Credible trade-off communication discloses assumptions and costs of each option, rather than selling one view (general practice)."),
    S("cm015", "comm", "The team will route simple requests to a smaller, cheaper model. Which framing suits the finance director best?", ["The share of requests that each model will handle, shown as a percentage split by week", "How a router component differs from a single model call, shown on an architecture diagram", "A summary of the safety evaluations that were run against each of the candidate models", "Cost per resolved case against manual cost, plus a quality check against regressions"], 3, "The same decision should be framed in each audience's decision terms; for finance that is unit cost and risk (general practice)."),
    S("cm016", "comm", "A sponsor asks for a firm monthly cost figure, but usage volumes are uncertain. What is the best answer?", ["Give one precise figure calculated from list prices and the highest volume anyone has mentioned so far", "Decline to estimate until the system has been running in production for a complete financial quarter", "Give a range from volume scenarios and sampled token usage, list the key assumptions and set a review point", "Quote the cost of the cheapest model, since the project can always switch to it if that is needed"], 2, "Costs depend on token volumes, which should be measured on sample data and presented as scenarios with assumptions (general practice; token counts are measurable via Anthropic's token counting)."),
    S("cm017", "comm", "Compliance wants every triage output reviewed by a person, while operations wants none. The architect proposes sampling plus mandatory review for high-impact or low-confidence classes. Which step most helps get agreement?", ["Show per-category risk and measured accuracy from the labelled sample behind each threshold", "Escalate to the steering committee immediately, with each side's written position and no analysis", "Implement the proposal and report the review rates each month after go-live has taken place", "Agree to full review at launch and promise to reduce it later without defining how that happens"], 0, "Agreement on risk controls follows from shared evidence by category, not from positions or open-ended promises (general practice).", "scCM1"),
    S("cm018", "comm", "A user-facing assistant could use a larger, slower model or a smaller, faster one. How should the architect communicate the recommendation?", ["Recommend the larger model because quality is the most important factor in every deployment", "Describe the choice as a technical detail that the engineering team will settle on its own once the build starts", "Present measured results of both on the agreed eval and the latency users feel, and name the deciding need", "Show only generic vendor guidance about how each size of model usually behaves in practice"], 2, "Anthropic's model-selection guidance asks teams to weigh quality, latency and cost against their own requirements and tests; communicate the same way."),
    S("cm019", "comm", "How should an architect explain a known limitation, such as occasional incorrect answers, to the business owner of an assistant?", ["State that the system is highly accurate and leave details of rare errors to the support team", "Describe the failure in business terms, its measured rate, how it is detected and the fallback", "Explain the limitation through transformer internals so that its cause is fully understood by the team", "Place a general disclaimer in the user interface and treat the matter as handled from then on"], 1, "Limitations should be communicated as measured risk with detection and fallback, not as reassurance or jargon (general practice)."),
    S("cm020", "comm", "After a design review, new test results show that the chosen multi-step agent design is more complex than the problem needs and a simple workflow would meet the targets. What is the best way to handle this?", ["Reopen the design decision openly with the new evidence and the effect on schedule, cost and risk", "Switch to the simpler workflow quietly, and mention the change in the next progress report", "Keep the agent design anyway, to avoid appearing to change direction in front of the client", "Delay the discussion until after go-live, so that the delivery schedule is not disrupted"], 0, "Anthropic's guidance on building agents is to find the simplest solution that works and add complexity only when it demonstrably helps; when evidence shows a simpler pattern suffices, revisit the decision openly (Anthropic guidance plus general practice)."),
    S("cm021", "comm", "The operations head wants a contract clause for \"99.9% availability and 99% accuracy\" for the triage service. What should the architect insist on first?", ["Accept both figures as written in the clause, to keep the relationship with the customer positive", "Remove the accuracy figure altogether, because accuracy cannot be placed in any kind of contract", "Describe the figures as targets with no consequences if they are missed, to avoid any disputes later", "Define each metric with its measurement, scope and exclusions, and check upstream limits"], 3, "SLA metrics need definitions, measurement and scope, and must reflect dependencies' real limits (general practice). Specific contract wording is a matter for Legal.", "scCM1"),
    S("cm022", "comm", "Which is the best-formed quality target for the triage assistant's service-level objective?", ["On the agreed 300-claim labelled set, at least 92 percent correct, re-measured monthly on fresh samples", "The assistant should classify claims correctly in the great majority of cases handled each month", "Claims staff should find the assistant's suggestions helpful in their day-to-day work routines", "The model used must be the most accurate Claude model that is available at any given time, whatever it costs"], 0, "A usable objective ties a metric, threshold, dataset and cadence together (general practice).", "scCM1"),
    S("cm023", "comm", "During weekly pilot demos, stakeholders keep adding new requests. What is the best way to manage them?", ["Log each one in a prioritised backlog and agree scope, time or budget changes with the sponsor", "Accept every request into the next sprint so that stakeholders feel heard and stay engaged throughout", "Decline all new requests until the pilot is over, and give stakeholders no explanation for the refusal", "Let the engineers choose which requests to build, based mainly on what they find technically interesting"], 0, "Scope growth is managed through a prioritised backlog and sponsor-agreed trade-offs (general practice).", "scCM1"),
    M("cm024", "comm", 3, "Which THREE elements make up a working feedback loop for a deployed assistant?", ["Automatic production prompt changes applied after every negative rating", "A way for users to flag poor answers, linked to the logged request", "A review of feedback once a year at the contract renewal meeting", "Regular review of sampled outputs by domain experts against a rubric", "Counting only positive ratings, so the metric reflects the successes", "A named owner and cadence for turning feedback into tested changes"], [1, 3, 5], "A feedback loop needs capture, expert sampling and an owned process to act on findings, with tested changes (general practice)."),
    S("cm025", "comm", "Pilot accuracy is 94 percent against a 95 percent target, and the measured human baseline is 91 percent. How should the result be presented?", ["Report that the target was missed and recommend that the project be stopped without further analysis", "Round the figure up to 95 percent so that the pilot is recorded as having met its original target", "Present only the human baseline comparison, since it makes the result look better to the audience", "Report against target and human baseline, show errors by category, and let the sponsors decide"], 3, "Honest reporting shows results against every agreed reference point and lets the decision owner decide (general practice)."),
    S("cm026", "comm", "Stakeholders saw a flawless demo and now expect the same performance in production. What best resets expectations?", ["Run more demos using the strongest examples so that confidence stays high among the stakeholders", "Tell stakeholders that demos are unreliable and avoid showing any further output to them for the time being", "Wait for production feedback and handle any dissatisfaction as and when it arises in practice", "Show results on typical and hard cases from the eval set, failures included, and define good enough"], 3, "Expectation resets use representative results, including failures, and an agreed acceptance line (general practice)."),
    S("cm027", "comm", "A client wants a commitment to response times, but traffic spikes could trigger rate-limit errors from the model API. Which commitment is most sound?", ["Commit to the targets unconditionally, on the basis that the API will scale to any request volume", "Avoid any time commitment, because the use of external APIs makes timing impossible to promise", "Make targets conditional on agreed volume and plan retries and headroom after checking limits", "Promise targets based on the lowest latency that was observed at any point during the testing phase"], 2, "Anthropic documents organisation-level rate limits by usage tier, with 429 errors when exceeded (and overloaded errors at times); commitments should be conditional on volume and supported by retry and capacity design."),
    S("cm028", "comm", "Monitoring shows a drop in output quality after a change to the retrieval data. Which communication approach is best?", ["Fix it quietly and mention it only if a stakeholder happens to ask about it at some later point", "Notify affected stakeholders by the agreed path with impact and mitigation, then run a root-cause review", "Wait until the monthly report so that the information arrives together with the other updates", "Send a broad notice to everyone in the company containing a technical explanation of the fault and its cause"], 1, "Incident communication should follow an agreed path, state impact and mitigation, and be followed by a review (general practice)."),
    S("cm029", "comm", "A client has no agreed routine for changes to the assistant after launch, and stakeholders send requests by email to different engineers. What is the best improvement?", ["Ask stakeholders to send all requests to the most senior engineer on the team and nobody else", "Set up one intake route and a regular change review with the business owner for visible priorities", "Make changes immediately whenever requested, to keep stakeholders satisfied with the response", "Stop accepting any changes after launch until the next annual planning cycle comes around again next year"], 1, "Post-launch change needs a single intake and a prioritising forum (general practice)."),
    S("cm030", "comm", "A pilot ends and sponsors disagree about whether it succeeded. What is the most likely root cause, and the best fix for the next pilot?", ["The model was too weak; start the next pilot with the largest available model on the market", "The sponsors were not technical enough; train them on how the models work before the next pilot", "Success criteria were never agreed in writing; set measurable exit criteria and a decision date and owner at kickoff", "The pilot was too short; extend it until the results look good to everyone who is involved in it, then decide"], 2, "Disputes about pilot success usually trace back to missing exit criteria (general practice)."),
    S("cm031", "comm", "The build team is handing the knowledge assistant to the client's operations team. Which document most helps them run it?", ["A design history written in date order, describing each week of the build and what was changed", "A runbook covering monitoring, common failures, index refresh, rollback and escalation contacts", "The source code repository alone, since developers can read the code to see what it does in practice", "A slide deck of the original business case, to remind the operators why it was built in the first place"], 1, "Operations handoff needs task-oriented runbook content, not history or code alone (general practice).", "scCM2"),
    S("cm032", "comm", "What should the handoff documentation say about the assistant's prompts?", ["Where they live in version control, the current version, model ID, parameters and passed eval results", "The final text of the prompt placed in an appendix, with no information about which version it is", "Only a note that prompts must not be edited without first consulting the original developers", "A list of every wording tried in development, ranked by how good each looked in the demos"], 0, "Prompts, model ID and parameters should be versioned and documented together with their eval results (general practice).", "scCM2"),
    S("cm033", "comm", "A solution must be explained to executives and to engineers. Which diagramming approach works best?", ["One detailed diagram containing every component in the system, so that nobody misses anything at all", "Layered views: a context view for the business and a data-flow view for engineers, each labelled", "Text only, because diagrams can be misread and a written description is considered more precise", "A photograph of the whiteboard sketch from the design session, kept in the project folder for reference"], 1, "Architecture documentation should offer audience-appropriate views of the same system (general practice)."),
    M("cm034", "comm", 2, "Which TWO items should the handoff pack include so that the operations team can judge whether a change is safe?", ["The complete chat history of the build team's discussion channel", "Every prompt variant tried and discarded during development", "The eval set, the scoring method and the latest baseline results", "The developer's personal API key, to allow quick testing", "Known limitations and failure modes with their mitigations"], [2, 4], "Safe change needs the eval baseline and a clear record of limitations; credentials and raw history do not help (general practice).", "scCM2"),
    S("cm035", "comm", "Several client teams will call Claude from their own services. Which shared implementation guidance is most valuable?", ["A request that each team keep its own private notes on how it calls the API within its own services", "A rule that every call must go through one central team's manual approval before it can be made", "Standards for server-side keys, retries, logging and prompt changes, with a reference implementation", "A list of favourite prompt phrases that teams can copy into their own services and then adapt as they wish"], 2, "Implementation guidance works best as concrete standards with a reference implementation (general practice)."),
    S("cm036", "comm", "A privacy review is planned for a new assistant that processes customer messages. What documentation should the architect prepare?", ["A data-flow record of what reaches the model, where it is stored and who owns it, for the DPO", "A statement that the assistant is compliant with relevant regulations, to save time in the review", "A summary of the model's training approach copied from public marketing material about the product", "Nothing until after launch, when real usage data can be reviewed by the privacy team at that point"], 0, "Provide a data-flow record and involve the DPO; legal conclusions are for Legal and Compliance (general practice, no legal advice)."),
    S("cm037", "comm", "The documentation for an assistant is out of date within weeks of launch. What is the best remedy?", ["Ask the original authors to rewrite the whole set of documents at the start of every quarter, in full", "Store the documents in a separate wiki owned and maintained by a dedicated documentation team", "Keep docs with the code, update them in the same change that alters system behaviour, and review them often", "Stop documenting details at all, since the behaviour of the model is going to change anyway"], 2, "Documentation stays accurate when updates are part of the same change and are reviewed on a cadence (general practice)."),
    S("cm038", "comm", "Why should an architecture document record the alternatives that were rejected?", ["So that the document looks thorough to external auditors, regardless of its practical use", "So that the original team can show to later reviewers that its choice was the only reasonable one", "So later teams avoid repeating experiments and can see which assumptions would change the decision", "So that every rejected option can be rebuilt quickly if the sponsor later changes their mind"], 2, "Rejected alternatives and their reasons guide future decisions and prevent repeated experiments (general practice)."),
    S("cm039", "comm", "A new developer must add a new document type to the assistant's knowledge base. Which guide helps most?", ["A reference of every configuration option, listed alphabetically by setting name", "A worked how-to covering adding the source, re-indexing and running the eval set", "A short note advising the developer to ask the original architect for help when stuck", "A recording of the entire original design workshop with the architecture team"], 1, "Task-focused how-to guides that include the test step let others extend a system safely (general practice)."),
    S("cm040", "comm", "When should the team first draft evaluation criteria for a Claude-based solution?", ["After the first release, once there are enough real user logs to examine", "During handoff, so the operations team can write down what they expect", "Only when stakeholders start to complain about quality in production", "In discovery and design, before building, as they define success"], 3, "Anthropic's testing guidance has teams define success criteria and test cases before engineering the prompt; they are drafted in discovery and design."),
    S("cm041", "comm", "The client's operations team will own the assistant, and the model it uses has just been listed as deprecated with a retirement date nine months away. What belongs in the handoff?", ["Add a note that models are managed by Anthropic and that the client need take no action at all", "Instruct the team to switch to an unpinned alias, so that retirements are then handled automatically", "Record the model ID, status and retirement date in the runbook, with a named owner to test migration in time", "Instruct the team to wait for the retirement date so that the very latest replacement can be chosen then"], 2, "Anthropic's model lifecycle uses statuses and retirement dates; retired models fail, so migration needs an owner and testing.", "scCM2"),
    S("cm042", "comm", "Three months after handoff, answers worsen after a bulk document refresh. Which monitoring practice would most likely have caught it early?", ["Scoring sampled live answers against the rubric, and checking retrieval after each document refresh", "Alerting on API error rates and latency, since errors show that something has failed", "Reviewing the monthly invoice for unusual cost, because quality changes usually affect spend", "Asking developers to test a handful of questions before each weekly team meeting"], 0, "Monitoring must cover answer quality and retrieval, not just uptime; this follows standard observability practice for LLM systems.", "scCM2"),
    S("cm043", "comm", "The operations team asks when they should re-run the full evaluation. What is the best guidance?", ["Only when the model provider announces that a new model has been released to its customers", "Once a year at the contract review, in order to keep the cost of the testing as low as possible", "Never, provided that the first evaluation passed with a good score at the time of launch", "On any change to prompt, model, data, retrieval or tools, and on a regular schedule because inputs drift"], 3, "Re-evaluate on every behaviour-affecting change and on a cadence, as inputs drift (general practice).", "scCM2"),
    S("cm044", "comm", "What is the best set of exit criteria for moving a pilot into production?", ["The sponsor's enthusiasm after the final demonstration, shown in the feedback given at the session", "Quality criteria met on the eval set, operations ready and a named owner's sign-off", "Completion of the planned engineering tasks on schedule, as recorded in the project plan tracker", "A clean result on the five examples that the team used while developing the first working version"], 1, "Production gates should combine quality evidence, operational readiness and ownership (general practice)."),
    M("cm045", "comm", 2, "Which TWO events should trigger evaluation work on a live Claude-based service?", ["The client opens a new regional office in another city next quarter", "Anthropic announces a retirement date for the service's model", "Marketing refreshes the colour scheme of the client's public website", "The source documents the assistant retrieves from are restructured", "The team shortens its sprint length from three weeks to two weeks"], [1, 3], "A retirement date forces a migration that must be evaluated on your own evals, and changed source data can alter retrieval and answers; unrelated organisational events do not (general practice with Anthropic's model lifecycle)."),
    S("cm046", "comm", "A team has twelve improvement ideas for the live assistant. How should they be prioritised?", ["Start with the ideas suggested by the most senior stakeholder, as those carry the most weight", "Do the technically most interesting ideas first, since they build team skills while delivering", "Rank them by expected effect on agreed measures, using logged failures, against effort", "Take them in the order proposed, so that nobody feels overlooked"], 2, "Iteration should be prioritised by evidence of impact on agreed measures versus effort (general practice)."),
    S("cm047", "comm", "The client will replace the assistant with a new system in a year. Which activity avoids orphaned data and dependencies?", ["A retirement plan for data deletion, notice to dependants, key revocation and archived documentation", "Switching the service off on the replacement's launch day with a notice posted on the intranet", "Leaving the service running indefinitely at low priority, in case it should be needed again at some point", "Deleting the source repository first so that nobody can restart the old system by accident"], 0, "Retirement is a lifecycle phase that needs a plan for data, dependents, credentials and records (general practice)."),
    S("cm048", "comm", "A new Claude model is released and a stakeholder wants an immediate upgrade. What is the best response?", ["Upgrade at once because newer models are always better on every measure that stakeholders care about", "Refuse any upgrade at all, because stability matters more than any gains that a new model may offer", "Upgrade only the development environment and assume that production traffic is covered by that test", "Treat it as a change: run evals on the candidate, canary some traffic and keep rollback ready"], 3, "Anthropic's guidance is to build evals specific to your use case and measure candidate models on your own workload; staged rollout and rollback are general practice for any behaviour change.", "scCM2"),
    M("cm049", "comm", 2, "Which TWO activities belong to the handoff phase rather than discovery?", ["Walking operators through the runbook and the common incidents", "Interviewing end users to gather the initial requirements for the service", "Drafting the first business case and the success measures for the pilot", "Confirming owners for monitoring, on-call support and model migration", "Choosing the first model to prototype with, based on early experiments"], [0, 3], "Handoff concerns transferring knowledge and ownership; requirements and business case belong to discovery (general practice).")
  );

  Object.assign(window.CCA.why, {
    "cm001": ["Starting from the problem and a measured baseline gives the project a target and a way to show value, which is the core of structured discovery.", "A demo built before the problem is defined tends to anchor expectations on features, not outcomes, and gives nothing to measure against.", "Competitor features say little about this client's problem, data or constraints, so the list drives scope without evidence of value.", "Prompt iteration is useful later, but without defined goals and test cases there is no way to tell whether a prompt is improving."],
    "cm002": ["Probing cost of error and the real latency need turns slogans into testable requirements that can be traded off, which is standard requirements elicitation.", "Adopting unmeasured targets as written sets up a failure, and a larger model usually costs more and responds more slowly, which works against \"instant\".", "Dismissing the target ends the conversation without finding out what the business needs, and it may be achievable for some categories with review.", "Setting targets after seeing results invites goalposts to move and removes the chance to design around real constraints."],
    "cm003": ["Data residency and auditability constrain hosting, logging and review design, so they directly shape the architecture.", "Visual styling is easy to change late and does not affect the design of the data flow or the controls.", "Understanding the current process and its metrics gives a baseline and reveals integration points, exceptions and what improvement means.", "Past vendors rarely decide how this solution should be built; the current systems it must integrate with matter far more.", "Meeting invitations are project administration and say nothing about requirements or constraints."],
    "cm004": ["Silently favouring one group leaves the other's need unaddressed and usually produces resistance or rework at launch.", "A fixed sample ignores which outputs are risky, so it satisfies neither group and is not tied to any evidence.", "Building both doubles cost and defers the decision, and users cannot weigh a risk trade-off that the business must own.", "Surfacing the trade-off with evidence and asking the accountable owner to rank priorities resolves the conflict transparently."],
    "cm005": ["Words such as quickly and noticeable are not measurable, so two reviewers could disagree on whether it is met.", "This prescribes a design choice, not a requirement, and it does not say what response time is acceptable.", "It names a metric, a threshold, a percentile and a load condition, so it can be tested and traced.", "A review process is not a requirement; it sets no threshold and leaves the team without a design target."],
    "cm006": ["An unowned assumption about access is a common cause of late slippage, and nothing in the plan would flag it.", "Hiding a workaround from stakeholders removes the chance to agree it with the data owner and creates security and compliance exposure.", "Silent descoping changes what the solution can do and breaks trust when the missing capability is discovered.", "Making the gap visible, assigning ownership and sequencing delivery around what exists keeps plans honest."],
    "cm007": ["Public benchmarks can inform what is achievable, but they measure other tasks and data and do not predict performance on this client's claims.", "A labelled sample provides both a baseline to compare against and the beginnings of an eval set, which is also how Anthropic's testing guidance frames success criteria.", "An unattainable target guarantees a missed commitment and ignores that humans also make errors on this task.", "A model grading itself on unlabelled data is not independent ground truth, so the targets would rest on unverified numbers."],
    "cm008": ["Confidence without evidence on this client's data and regulatory context risks committing to something that cannot be delivered.", "A bounded test with agreed criteria turns an open question into evidence at low cost before any commitment is made.", "A blanket refusal skips the analysis of risk and controls; many regulated processes can use LLMs with appropriate oversight.", "Other firms' demos use different data and controls and do not show how the solution would perform here."],
    "cm009": ["Raw transcripts are test artefacts, not requirements, and bury the few facts that matter.", "Linking requirements to goals shows what is lost if a requirement changes and supports prioritisation.", "Branding may belong in a design guide but it is not what lets teams trace and assess requirement changes.", "A criterion makes the requirement testable and an owner makes someone accountable for its change.", "Attendance may be useful as project record but does not support traceability of requirements."],
    "cm010": ["Extra history only changes the context sent in a request; it does not retrain the model, so the sponsor's plan would never deliver gains.", "There is no automatic improvement period; this repeats the misconception with a delay and sets up a disappointment.", "The Messages API is stateless, the model's weights do not change from your requests, and Anthropic does not train on commercial inputs or outputs by default, so improvement is a managed process that must be resourced.", "Leaving a wrong assumption in place makes the later gap larger and damages trust once it surfaces."],
    "cm011": ["Leaderboard positions and size are hard for a sponsor to interpret and do not reflect this project's data or constraints.", "Hiding alternatives prevents the sponsor from understanding the trade-offs and weakens the credibility of the recommendation.", "Leaving a sponsor to dig out the decision points from raw detail shifts the communication job back to them.", "Outcome measures on the sponsor's own cases let a non-specialist weigh the options and see the reasoning behind the recommendation."],
    "cm012": ["The bare choice does not explain the reasoning, so later teams cannot tell whether conditions have changed.", "Recording why a choice was made, and what was knowingly given up, lets later teams judge whether it still holds.", "Approvals are useful for audit but do not explain the reasoning behind the decision.", "Marketing material is generic and does not capture this project's context, criteria or trade-offs."],
    "cm013": ["An absolute stance that ignores the data gives the sponsor no path forward and is not tied to evidence from this pilot.", "Using the sponsor's own data on cost of error to propose a graduated, evidence-driven control addresses the concern while keeping the benefit.", "Deploying a known risk without agreement exposes the organisation to losses that the pilot already hinted at.", "This is not accurate; the issue is risk by value band, and an overstatement erodes the architect's credibility."],
    "cm014": ["Showing how numbers were produced lets stakeholders judge their reliability and prevents false precision.", "A single option leaves stakeholders unable to see that a decision was made, or what else was possible.", "Best-case figures overstate expected outcomes and make later results look like a failure.", "Omitting risks undermines trust when they surface and removes stakeholders' chance to help mitigate them.", "Exposing the cost of each option is what makes the comparison a real trade-off and builds credibility."],
    "cm015": ["A routing split describes mechanics and does not show savings or whether quality is protected.", "Architecture detail is relevant to engineers but gives finance nothing to evaluate.", "Safety results matter to risk and security audiences, but they do not answer the finance question about cost.", "Finance cares about unit economics and risk-adjusted savings, so cost per outcome with a quality guard speaks to that."],
    "cm016": ["A single number conveys false precision and may rely on the wrong volume and token assumptions.", "Refusing to estimate blocks the funding decision, and a reasoned range is possible from a sample and scenarios.", "A range built on measured token use and explicit assumptions is honest about uncertainty and can be tightened with real usage.", "Quoting the cheapest option hides the cost of the model the project really needs, and switching may reduce quality."],
    "cm017": ["Evidence by category lets both sides see why the thresholds sit where they do and challenge the numbers rather than positions.", "Escalating without analysis passes the decision upward without the information needed to make it well.", "Reporting after launch gives no chance to agree thresholds beforehand and may entrench the conflict.", "An undefined promise to reduce review gives operations no criteria and may never be honoured."],
    "cm018": ["A universal ranking of qualities ignores this project's latency requirement, which the user experience may depend on.", "Model choice affects cost, speed and quality that stakeholders care about, so it belongs in the conversation.", "Evidence on the agreed measures plus the deciding requirement makes the reasoning transparent and challengeable.", "Generic guidance does not show how the models behave on this project's tasks, so it cannot support a decision."],
    "cm019": ["Downplaying errors leaves the owner unprepared and eroding trust when failures occur.", "Rate, detection and fallback let the owner judge the residual risk and plan for it, which is a mature way to discuss limitations.", "Technical causes do not help the owner decide what to do about the risk.", "A disclaimer does not detect or correct errors and does not inform the owner's risk decisions."],
    "cm020": ["Transparent revision shows evidence-based judgement and lets stakeholders reassess with the new information.", "Silent changes undermine trust and bypass the people who approved the original design.", "Keeping a design that evidence no longer supports adds cost and risk to protect appearances.", "Waiting locks in the extra complexity and its running cost, so the issue becomes more expensive to fix."],
    "cm021": ["Committing to undefined figures turns a relationship asset into a dispute when the first measurement is disputed.", "Quality objectives can be defined against an agreed evaluation set; the answer is to define them well, not drop them.", "A clause without consequences gives the customer little reason to trust it and does not remove the need for definitions.", "An SLA is only meaningful if each metric is measurable and scoped, and dependencies' limits must be understood before committing; the final clause wording then goes to Legal."],
    "cm022": ["It names the data, metric, threshold and cadence, and refreshes the sample so the number stays honest.", "\"Great majority\" cannot be tested, so a disagreement cannot be settled objectively.", "Perceived helpfulness is worth tracking but is subjective and does not define a pass or fail line.", "This fixes an implementation and may conflict with cost and latency, without stating an accuracy requirement."],
    "cm023": ["A visible backlog with explicit trade-offs keeps expectations aligned and gives stakeholders a fair path for their ideas.", "Accepting everything silently stretches the schedule and dilutes the pilot's ability to prove its core value.", "Blanket refusals with no reasoning damage engagement and may discard genuinely valuable ideas.", "Engineering interest is not a prioritisation criterion; the sponsor owns trade-offs on scope and value."],
    "cm024": ["Unreviewed automatic changes can regress other cases, so changes should be tested before release.", "Linked flags let the team find and reproduce the failing cases.", "Annual review is too slow to catch and correct problems while they are affecting users.", "Sampling catches quality problems users do not report and keeps the rubric consistent.", "Ignoring negative signals removes the information needed to improve.", "Without an owner and rhythm, feedback piles up unused; the loop only closes when someone acts on it."],
    "cm025": ["A single missed percentage point does not decide viability when the system exceeds the human baseline.", "Rounding to hit the target misrepresents results and destroys credibility when the figure is questioned.", "Selecting favourable comparisons hides the target gap and undermines trust in the reporting.", "Showing both comparisons and where errors fall gives stakeholders what they need to make an informed acceptance decision."],
    "cm026": ["More curated demos reinforce the unrealistic expectation and increase the later disappointment.", "Withholding output removes the shared evidence needed to build realistic trust.", "Waiting lets the gap between expectation and reality become a complaint rather than a managed conversation.", "Showing the real distribution of results, with failures, replaces an anecdote with a shared understanding of expected performance."],
    "cm027": ["Rate limits exist and exceeding them returns 429 errors, so an unconditional promise carries unmanaged risk.", "Timing can be committed conditionally with design for retries and capacity, so refusing outright is unnecessary.", "API rate limits apply to the organisation and depend on its usage tier, so a response-time commitment must be tied to agreed volume and backed by retry and capacity planning.", "Best-case latency ignores load, retries and variance, so it will not hold at the percentiles that matter."],
    "cm028": ["Concealed incidents damage trust severely when discovered and prevent stakeholders from managing their own risk.", "Prompt, structured notification builds trust, and a follow-up review shows that the process learns from incidents.", "Delaying notice leaves stakeholders exposed to bad outputs and ignores the escalation expectations agreed earlier.", "An untargeted technical broadcast overwhelms people who are not affected and does not say what they should do."],
    "cm029": ["A single person becomes a bottleneck and single point of failure, and there is still no prioritisation or visibility.", "A single route and a regular review give stakeholders a predictable path and the team a prioritised queue.", "Immediate unreviewed changes bypass regression testing and risk assessment for the sake of responsiveness.", "Freezing for a year ignores rapidly changing data and models and frustrates users with unresolved problems."],
    "cm030": ["Model strength does not explain disagreement about what success meant, and the largest model is not always best.", "Training may help, but the disagreement is about definition of success, not understanding of the technology.", "Without pre-agreed criteria, each person judges success by their own standard, so the fix is to set them at kickoff.", "Extending without criteria only postpones the disagreement and encourages stopping when results look good."],
    "cm031": ["A chronological history is hard to use in an incident because operators cannot find the steps they need.", "Operators need task-oriented steps for the situations they will meet, which a runbook provides.", "Code alone does not tell operators what to monitor, what normal looks like or whom to call.", "The business case explains purpose but gives no operational guidance."],
    "cm032": ["Prompt, model and parameters behave as one release unit, so recording them together with test results enables safe change and rollback.", "Without versions and the evals they passed, operators cannot tell whether a change is an improvement or roll back.", "A prohibition without the means to test and change safely leaves the client dependent and the system unmaintainable.", "A ranked list of demo impressions is not evidence and clutters the documentation with irrelevant history."],
    "cm033": ["A single dense diagram overwhelms executives and still tends to omit the flow engineers need.", "Different audiences need different levels of detail, and layered views keep each readable while staying consistent.", "Text alone makes structure and flow harder to grasp, and lengthy prose is rarely read by executives.", "Whiteboard photos are informal, hard to maintain and not suitable as a shared record."],
    "cm034": ["A raw chat history is large, unstructured and hard to search, so it will not help judge safety.", "Discarded variants add noise; the useful knowledge is why decisions were made, not every trial.", "A change can only be judged by re-running the same eval and comparing it with the recorded baseline.", "Personal credentials must never be handed over; operators should use their own keys held securely.", "Knowing where the system is weak tells operators what to watch for and what to retest after a change."],
    "cm035": ["Private notes lead to divergent practices and no shared learning or control.", "Manual approval for every call creates a bottleneck and does not scale with the number of teams.", "A short set of standards plus a working example reduces inconsistent, insecure integrations across teams.", "Prompt phrases without the engineering standards do not address security, resilience or change control."],
    "cm036": ["A clear data-flow record supports the privacy review, and involving the DPO is the right route for regulated data.", "Compliance is for the DPO and Legal to determine; an architect's assertion without review is not valid.", "Training descriptions do not show how this system handles this client's data.", "Privacy review should precede launch, because data handling is a design decision that is costly to change later."],
    "cm037": ["A periodic rewrite is expensive and still leaves long gaps in which the docs are wrong.", "Separation from the code makes it easy for changes to be made without a matching update.", "Tying docs to the change that makes them stale, and reviewing them periodically, keeps them current.", "Behaviour that changes is the reason to document decisions and limits; having no documentation harms operability."],
    "cm038": ["Thoroughness for appearance has no operational value, while recorded reasoning does.", "Documentation should enable future judgement, not defend past choices.", "Recording rejected options and why saves rework and shows when circumstances might justify reopening them.", "The goal is to capture reasoning, not to maintain a build-ready version of every alternative."],
    "cm039": ["An alphabetical reference does not tell a newcomer what to do or in what order.", "A concrete worked example, with the test step included, enables safe extension without the original team.", "Dependence on one person is the opposite of a handoff and does not scale.", "A long workshop recording is hard to search and contains discussion rather than steps."],
    "cm040": ["Logs are useful input later, but without earlier criteria the team builds without knowing the target.", "By handoff, the design has been fixed; criteria written then cannot influence it.", "Reactive criteria arrive after the harm and make the first comparison point the incident.", "Early criteria give design and build a target and prevent success being redefined after seeing results, matching Anthropic's advice to define success criteria first."],
    "cm041": ["Anthropic retires models on a published schedule; the client's service stops working if nobody migrates it.", "Per Anthropic, aliases (offered for earlier models) only point to the latest dated snapshot of a minor version; they do not move you to a new generation, and the model behind one can still be deprecated and retired.", "Anthropic notes that retired models fail requests, so the owner and the date must be explicit, and migration must be tested on the eval set.", "Waiting leaves no time to test and risks an outage when requests begin to fail."],
    "cm042": ["Quality sampling and post-refresh retrieval checks catch degradation that availability metrics cannot show.", "A degraded answer still returns a success status, so error rates and latency would stay normal.", "Cost may not move at all when retrieval quality falls, so the invoice is a weak quality signal.", "Informal spot checks are inconsistent and unlikely to cover the cases affected by a refresh."],
    "cm043": ["Many other changes, such as data or prompt edits, also alter behaviour and need checking.", "A yearly check leaves long periods when a regression could go unnoticed.", "A pass at launch does not guarantee quality after later changes or as the inputs evolve.", "Each of these changes can shift behaviour, and drift in real inputs means periodic checks are needed even without a change."],
    "cm044": ["Enthusiasm is not evidence of readiness and ignores operational and quality risks.", "Quality, operability and ownership together define production readiness; missing any one creates risk.", "Finishing tasks shows activity, not that quality targets or operational capability are met.", "A handful of development examples is too small and biased a sample to prove readiness."],
    "cm045": ["An office opening does not itself change the system's inputs or behaviour.", "A retirement date forces migration to another model, and Anthropic's guidance is to evaluate the replacement on your own tasks before it replaces the current one.", "A branding refresh has no effect on model behaviour, retrieval or prompts.", "Changes to retrieved content can alter answers and retrieval quality, so evaluation should be repeated.", "Process cadence does not change the system's behaviour, so no re-evaluation is needed on its own."],
    "cm046": ["Seniority is not evidence of value and can send the team to low-impact work.", "Interest is not a measure of user or business value.", "Impact on agreed measures, informed by actual failures, avoids effort spent on changes that matter little.", "Order of arrival ignores impact and effort, so the most valuable ideas may wait longest."],
    "cm047": ["A planned decommission closes the loose ends that otherwise leave data, credentials and consumers stranded.", "Abrupt shutdown can break dependent systems and leave data and credentials in place.", "A forgotten service continues to cost money and holds data and keys without an owner.", "Deleting the repository removes the documentation and history that decommissioning and audits need."],
    "cm048": ["Newer models may differ in behaviour, latency or cost, and need testing on your own tasks.", "Refusing changes ignores deprecation schedules and potential gains, and the need to move eventually.", "Development testing does not show how production traffic behaves.", "Anthropic's guidance is to test candidate models on your own evals; staged rollout limits the risk of an upgrade."],
    "cm049": ["Handoff transfers operating knowledge to the owners, and a walkthrough is a standard way of doing it.", "Eliciting requirements is a discovery activity that precedes design.", "Defining the business case and initial measures belongs to discovery.", "Clear ownership at handoff prevents the common gap where no one is responsible for the live system.", "Prototyping choices belong to design, well before handoff."]
  });
})();
